import{_ as t,o,c as r,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Internal call chains","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"dev/call-chains.md","filePath":"dev/call-chains.md"}'),s={name:"dev/call-chains.md"};function c(d,e,i,n,l,h){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="internal-call-chains" tabindex="-1">Internal call chains <a class="header-anchor" href="#internal-call-chains" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Internal call chains&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Six dissections of the paths that matter most when reading or changing the kernel. Each is presented as numbered stages with the trigger -&gt; processing -&gt; impact contract and the file that owns each stage. Addresses, constants and error strings are from source; nothing here is speculative.</p><h2 id="_1-the-execve-path-running-a-program" tabindex="-1">1. The execve path (running a program) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-execve-path-running-a-program" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. The execve path (running a program)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: ush parses a command line that is not a built-in (or a program calls <code>SYS_EXECVE</code> directly, or <code>job_create</code>/<code>run</code> wraps it).</p><ol><li><strong>ush dispatch</strong> (<code>ocos/l1apps/ush/main.c</code>): the line is split (<code>$VAR</code> expanded, pipes/redirection handled), argv assembled in static storage, <code>fork()</code> then <code>execve(name, argv)</code> in the child; the parent <code>wait4</code>s (foreground) or records a job slot (background <code>&amp;</code>).</li><li><strong>Syscall</strong> (<code>SYS_EXECVE</code> = 11, <code>core_usermode.c</code>): the name is looked up via <code>core_userprog_lookup(name, &amp;size)</code> - the shared name-to-embedded-ELF lookup over the generated <code>OC_USERPROGS[]</code> registry (<code>userprogs_data.h</code>, 284 entries, each its own gzip stream; ush alone stays raw because <code>OC_L1_APP_DECL</code> needs compile-time ELF pointers).</li><li><strong>Lazy gunzip</strong> (<code>core_usermode.c</code> + <code>core_lib_gzip.c</code>): on the FIRST exec of an app, the gzip blob is inflated into a per-program heap cache (<code>g_prog_cache[OC_USERPROGS_COUNT]</code>); later execs reuse the cached raw ELF. The inflater is a full RFC 1951/1952 implementation (stored/fixed/dynamic Huffman, resumable streaming feed, CRC32+ISIZE verification).</li><li><strong>ELF load</strong> (<code>core_usermode.c</code>): ET_EXEC is mapped at the fixed user layout (code base 0x400000); ET_DYN (PIE) routes to the loader family (<code>services/loader</code>): PT_INTERP is parsed, ld.so is mapped at the fixed 0x10000000, the main program plus known .so images at their bump bases, ASLR via <code>user_aslr_page_slice</code> (two rdtsc samples + splitmix64 finalizer), then control transfers to ld.so&#39;s entry - ld.so (ring 3) resolves .dynamic, patches GOT/PLT and jumps to <code>app_main</code>&#39;s caller <code>_start</code> (the shared <code>crt0.asm</code> reads <code>[rsp]</code>=argc / <code>[rsp+8]</code>=argv, aligns, calls <code>app_main</code>).</li><li><strong>Address-space construction</strong>: fresh PML4; kernel PML4[1..3] copied; PRIVATE PDPT[0]+PD0 (copy of the kernel 512 x 2 MiB huge PDEs); PD0[2] (0x400000-0x600000) replaced by an <em>empty</em> PT (never pre-populated with kernel phys pages); stack top 0x40000000 (64 KiB).</li><li><strong>Scheduling</strong>: the new task occupies one of 16 user proc slots / 32 task slots, priority 15 default; the parent&#39;s wait4 completes when the unified reaper (<code>user_process_reap_resources</code>) runs after exit - from both tick and yield, under cli (BUG-0282 double-free fix).</li></ol><p><strong>Impact</strong>: the first-syscall gate - the FIRST syscall from the default L1 entry (pid == <code>g_l1_entry_pid</code>) flips <code>OCST_STAGE_L1_READY</code>, the one-shot boot-state load on the hot path.</p><h2 id="_2-one-l1svc-call-end-to-end" tabindex="-1">2. One L1SVC call end to end <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-one-l1svc-call-end-to-end" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. One L1SVC call end to end&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: a ring-3 wrapper calls <code>syscall5(SYS_L1SVC, cat, op, arg, out, cap)</code>.</p><ol><li><strong>Ring-3 wrapper</strong> (<code>ocos/interface/l1_lib.h</code>): <code>l1svc_main</code> joins argv into a 256-byte arg line; <code>out</code> is a static 32 KiB BSS buffer.</li><li><strong>Entry</strong>: <code>int 0x80</code> -&gt; IDT vector 128 (DPL=3 gate) -&gt; stub pushes 15 GPRs + fake error code -&gt; <code>arch_isr_dispatch</code> -&gt; <code>core_syscall_dispatch(frame)</code>; <code>r8</code> (5th arg, cap) is stashed in <code>g_syscall_arg5</code>.</li><li><strong>sys_l1svc</strong> (<code>services/sysapi/src/svc_sysapi_syscall.c</code>): validates out/cap (1..32768, or the 0/0 fire-and-forget spelling), copies <code>arg</code> into the 256-byte kernel buffer (4096 for cat OCIRE), <code>sti</code>.</li><li><strong>Service dispatch</strong> (<code>services/svc_framework.c</code>): <code>svc_framework_dispatch(cat, op, arg, out, cap)</code> scans <code>s_svcs[64]</code> for the exact category string and calls the service&#39;s <code>l1_dispatch(op, arg, out, cap)</code>.</li><li><strong>kcap capture</strong> (<code>kernel/core_kcap.c</code>): captured-style ops run inside <code>core_kcap_run_fn(fn, arg, out, cap)</code> - the capture hook IS the console hook (one slot) while active: characters collect into <code>out</code>, framebuffer draw and the serial tee are suppressed, captures stack to 4 levels (only the innermost active capture accepts data), overflow is marked never silently truncated; the base hook (the boot serial tee installed by kmain) is reinstalled when the last capture ends.</li><li><strong>Return</strong>: <code>cli</code>; the service&#39;s own non-zero exit status is still a successful call - only an unrouteable cat/op (or empty out with rc&lt;0) returns -1; otherwise the NUL-terminated text is copied back and 0 returned. Signal delivery (if any) happens after the handler, before <code>iretq</code>.</li></ol><p><strong>Impact</strong>: this single door carries all 208 ops; the <code>sti/cli</code> window is what lets device-wait loops inside op bodies be tick-driven instead of freezing the machine.</p><h2 id="_3-the-network-receive-path" tabindex="-1">3. The network receive path <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-the-network-receive-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. The network receive path&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: a frame arrives at the NIC (or the 10 ms soft-timer fires the poll).</p><ol><li><strong>Driver layer</strong>: e1000 rings (<code>e1000_desc_t</code>, 2048-byte buffers) / virtio-net queues (<code>net_virtq_*</code>, VIRTIO_BUF_SIZE 2048) / the WP-10b framework cards expose <code>driver_nic_ops_t.recv(dev, buf, maxlen)</code> - polled, returning one frame (&gt;0), 0 when none, -1 on error.</li><li><strong>Poll engine</strong> (<code>services/net/src/svc_net_core.c</code>): <code>net_start_timer()</code> registers <code>core_timer_register_periodic(net_timer_cb, NULL, 10)</code> - <code>net_poll</code> every <strong>10 ms</strong>; <code>net_poll</code> is also called from thread context in <code>net_recv</code>&#39;s wait loops (socket reads block productively).</li><li><strong>Ethernet demux</strong> (<code>eth_hdr_t</code>, types ARP 0x0806 / IP 0x0800): ARP feeds the 16-entry cache (<code>g_arp_cache[ARP_CACHE_SIZE]</code>); IP continues.</li><li><strong>IP</strong> (<code>net_ip_hdr_t</code>): fragment reassembly (<code>IP_FRAG_MAX_FRAGS 8</code>, 8*1480 buffer, 5 s timeout; stats for reassembled/dropped/evicted - BUG-0210); bad-checksum datagrams are counted and dropped (<code>net_rx_bad_checksum</code>, BUG-0072).</li><li><strong>Netfilter INPUT chain</strong> (<code>g_nf_rules[NF_MAX_RULES=32]</code>, conntrack <code>g_nf_ct[32]</code>, 60 s timeout; ACCEPT/DROP/REJECT, NEW/ESTABLISHED): a DROP ends the walk here, counted.</li><li><strong>L4 demux</strong>: ICMP echo -&gt; ping responder; UDP -&gt; the 32-handler table (<code>g_udp_handlers</code>, DHCP 68/67, DNS 53 client); TCP -&gt; the 32-connection engine (<code>net_tcp_conn_t</code> on the kernel heap, ~520 KiB): sequencing, SACK blocks, out-of-order cache, CUBIC congestion window (RFC 8312 integer math, <code>cubic_state_t</code>), RTO with srtt/rttvar + Karn&#39;s rule, timestamps, window scaling.</li><li><strong>Socket</strong> (<code>g_sockets[MAX_SOCKETS=32]</code>, heap): <code>SYS_NETRECV</code> finds the socket&#39;s rx buffer (TCP rx_buf 8192 - was 2048 before WP-09-FIX BUG-002) and copies to the user buffer; the blocked reader wakes.</li></ol><p><strong>Impact</strong>: <code>netstat</code>/<code>tcpstats</code> read the very tables this chain mutates (<code>net_stats_t</code> counters, <code>g_tcp_conns[]</code> CUBIC internals); <code>net_e1000_resume()</code> replays legacy e1000 register programming after ACPI S3 resume.</p><h2 id="_4-the-block-i-o-path" tabindex="-1">4. The block I/O path <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-the-block-i-o-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. The block I/O path&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: a filesystem (or partition parser, or the installer) reads or writes sectors.</p><ol><li><strong>fs layer</strong> (services/fs): FAT32/exFAT/ext4 read metadata and data through <code>driver_block_read/write_sectors</code> (index form) or the pointer form <code>driver_block_read/write(dev, lba, count, buf)</code> - never directly to a driver.</li><li><strong>Cache gate</strong> (<code>services/block/src/svc_block_cache.c</code>): the 64-slot LRU write-back cache (<code>driver_block_cache_slot_t</code>, 32 KiB total) is consulted per (dev_idx, lba); hits update <code>last_used</code>; misses pick a victim - empty first, then LRU order, and a dirty victim is only released when its write-back SUCCEEDS (BUG-0116: the old code ignored the write-back return and silently dropped data); if every dirty slot fails, callers fall back to write-through.</li><li><strong>Device dispatch</strong> (<code>svc_block_drivers.c</code>): the device&#39;s <code>driver_block_ops_t.read/write</code> runs; <code>.flush</code> (optional) is invoked by <code>driver_block_flush(dev)</code> after pushing dirty sectors (ATA FLUSH CACHE / NVMe Flush / virtio-blk FLUSH).</li><li><strong>Driver</strong> (drivers/storage): ATA PIO LBA28/LBA48 or Bus-Master DMA (PRDT at BAR4, PIO fallback preserved); AHCI READ/WRITE DMA EXT through a per-port 4 KiB page-aligned bounce buffer split into chunks of at most 8 sectors (one PRDT entry per chunk); NVMe Read/Write (0x02/0x01) with 64-bit PRP over two I/O queue pairs; virtio-blk request/reply descriptors.</li><li><strong>DMA discipline</strong>: transfer buffers respect the heap&#39;s 32-byte header/16-byte alignment contract (the odd-parity 40-byte header bug shifted ATA-DMA buffers by 8); xHCI refuses DMA above 4 GiB at probe and per-transfer.</li><li><strong>Protection gate</strong> (OCIRE embed): when the embed is active, every write through a covering device is REFUSED; the kernel-internal <code>driver_block_read/write_sectors_trusted</code> is the only writer (the embed service and partition children); never callable from ring 3.</li></ol><p><strong>Impact</strong>: <code>sync</code> (op BLK 11) flushes this chain end-to-end; <code>dskstat</code>/<code>lsblk</code> report the per-device stats and cache counters; <code>power shutdown</code> flushes the cache before S5.</p><h2 id="_5-the-crash-path" tabindex="-1">5. The crash path <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-crash-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. The crash path&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: a ring-0 exception no handler claims, a <code>kernel_panic</code> call, an <code>OC_ASSERT</code> failure, or a watchdog policy PANIC.</p><ol><li><strong>Vector entry</strong> (<code>arch_idt_stub.S</code> -&gt; <code>arch_exceptions.c</code>): nested-#PF guard first (a #PF inside #PF handling prints <code>*** NESTED #PF INSIDE EXCEPTION HANDLER - HALTING ***</code> and cli;hlt); the L1 handler chain (4/vector, most-recently-registered first) runs; #PF routes to <code>mem_vmm_handle_page_fault</code> (stack growth, protection violations never remap); ring-3-origin faults take the SIGSEGV delivery / kill path instead (vectors 1/2/3 kills run on the dedicated exit stack, leaving IST2 intact - BUG-0139).</li><li><strong>Panic routing</strong> (<code>kernel_panic</code>/<code>kernel_panic_frame</code> -&gt; handlers installed by services/crash via <code>arch_set_panic_handlers</code>): #DF is special-cased ENTIRELY at dispatcher depth with one static 192-byte serial line (no record, no dump, no flush - stated in KNOWN_ISSUES); every other source takes the full path.</li><li><strong>Normalization + first record</strong>: CR3 switched to the kernel address space so traceback/dump/heap reads are safe; <code>klog_record(KLOG_FATAL, &quot;kernel&quot;, reason)</code> writes the ring (ring only - no VFS at depth).</li><li><strong>Banner + snapshot</strong> (<code>svc_crash_core.c</code>): serial-authoritative (VGA red best effort): <code>*** KERNEL PANIC: &lt;reason&gt; [task=&lt;name&gt;] ***</code>, the kernel identity line, <code>source: exception &lt;n&gt; (&lt;name&gt;) err=0x...</code>, task line, the register dump (4x4 grid r15..rax + rip/cs/ss/rflags/rsp/int/err + cr2 for #PF + cr3), rbp-chain + stack-scan traceback filtered by <code>looks_like_code</code>. Stack exhaustion is attributed: guard-page #PF or exhausted-stack #DF names the task in the reason.</li><li><strong>OCDUMP01 core dump</strong> (<code>core_dump_save</code>): fills the boot-reserved buffer (<code>core_dump_alloc()</code> ran kmalloc once at kmain step 16a - after this point kmalloc may fail or the heap may corrupt, the panic path only touches the reserved buffer): header, 22-u64 registers (frame or live snapshot), task block, 4 KiB stack window, CR3 + PML4 self-scan PTE count; persisted to <code>/var/crash/core.dump</code> (ramfs rootfs or FAT32); with NO filesystem the first 2048 bytes are hex-dumped to serial so data is not lost.</li><li><strong>Crashlog ring</strong> (<code>crashlog_record</code>): a <code>crashlog_entry_t</code> in the 32-slot ring (vector or synthetic source 32..36; ring 0/3; err/rip/rsp/cr2; tid/pid/task; jiffies + rtc_epoch); <code>crashlog_flush()</code> appends everything to <code>/var/log/crash.log</code>.</li><li><strong>Policy</strong>: registered panic handlers (up to 4, most-recently-registered first) may claim recovery (<code>*** panic handler claimed recovery - resuming ***</code>, <code>*** CONTAINED: ... - isolated, kernel continues ***</code> - the TUI-guard mechanism); otherwise <code>CRASH_POLICY_HALT</code> (default, cli + safe hlt) or <code>CRASH_POLICY_REBOOT</code> (8042 + 0xCF9), switchable live by <code>crashtest policy [halt|reboot]</code>.</li></ol><p><strong>OCBL/OCIRE relay (boot failures)</strong>: a hang that stops the boot-state heartbeats stops feeding the 6300ESB -&gt; hard reset -&gt; OCBL reads the 0x98000 state area, judges (silence &gt;= 8 s, no CLEAN marker), writes the BOOT_FAILED record at 0x9B000 -&gt; next boot auto-boots OCIRE -&gt; OCIRE reads the record and prints the stall point + repair advice. A <em>user-requested</em> OCIRE boot writes a USER_REQUEST record instead. Full contract: <a href="./data-structures.html">data-structures.md</a> section 8, <a href="./../internals/crash-dump.html">../internals/crash-dump.md</a>.</p><h2 id="_6-the-ota-install-path" tabindex="-1">6. The OTA install path <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-the-ota-install-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. The OTA install path&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Trigger</strong>: <code>update</code> (bare online flow), <code>update --local &lt;pkg&gt;</code> (offline), or the update wizard&#39;s step 5 confirm.</p><ol><li><strong>Check</strong> (<code>L1SVC_OTA_CHECKUPDATE</code> -&gt; <code>ota_update_check</code>): fetch <code>update.json</code> (HTTP or HTTPS per the configured <code>update_url</code>), compare <code>version</code> and <code>build</code> (<code>OC_KERNEL_BUILD</code>, numeric comparison - BUG-0347; the build id is <code>git rev-list --count HEAD + 21</code> so it stays monotonic above published ids). Anchors: &quot;Current version is up to date.&quot; / &quot;New version available.&quot;.</li><li><strong>Download</strong> (<code>ota_update_download</code>): streamed over TCP/TLS to <code>/data/update-pkg.tar.gz</code> (default; tmp <code>/tmp/update-pkg.tar.gz</code>); failures print <code>&quot;update: download failed: &quot;</code>.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong>: package SHA256 against the manifest (<code>update_verify</code>); the manifest also carries the payload sha256 over concatenated member bodies in archive order.</li><li><strong>Decode</strong>: gunzip (RFC 1952/1951, CRC32+ISIZE verified) + untar (streaming ustar with checksum verification, GNU &#39;L&#39; long names, pax skip) directly into the <strong>inactive slot</strong> (slot B when running A; <code>ota_ab_target_slot()</code> - &quot;update: cannot determine target slot (not booted from A/B?)&quot; when running from ISO).</li><li><strong>Write boot flags</strong>: <code>/boot/next_B</code> created; the new slot&#39;s kernel writes <code>/boot/bootfail_B</code> <strong>pessimistically at boot</strong> and removes it only when the system is fully up (<code>ota_update_confirm_boot</code> at kmain step 16c); <code>ok_B</code> marks a confirmed B. Boot selection (grub.cfg): <code>next_B</code> present AND <code>bootfail_B</code> absent -&gt; boot B; both present -&gt; ROLLBACK to A; <code>ok_B</code> -&gt; B; otherwise A. Boot-attempt budget: GRUB env block <code>/boot/grub/grubenv</code> (1024 bytes, <code>ab_attempts</code>), <code>OC_AB_MAX_ATTEMPTS 3</code>, reset when A finishes booting or B confirms.</li><li><strong>Reboot</strong>: the flow prints &quot;reboot required&quot;; <code>POWER/REBOOT</code> (8042 + 0xCF9) lands on the new slot; rollback (<code>L1SVC_OTA_ROLLBACK</code>) clears the flags.</li></ol><p><strong>Impact</strong>: a failed write never touches the running slot - the active system keeps serving until the flip; the A/B disk layout (p1 boot/flags 32 MB FAT32 + p2 slot A 128M + p3 slot B 128M + p4 /data) is created by <code>abdisk</code>, which re-runs <code>ota_ab_rescan()</code> afterwards. Full page: <a href="./../internals/ota-ab.html">../internals/ota-ab.md</a>.</p>',26)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",c]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
